 3. Microsoft: &quot;People matter more than AI&quot; 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Axios Visuals. Photo: Penguin Random House

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Microsoft AI chief Mustafa Suleyman says the race to build ever more powerful AI needs a red line: AI must remain under human control.

 Why it matters : Microsoft is taking a harder line than rivals on AI design when there is growing concern that the industry's current path could threaten humanity.

 What they're saying: That could mean sacrificing capabilities others are willing to pursue. 

 "I think some people think that it's inevitable that superintelligence is going to exceed human control," Suleyman told Axios. "But I don't agree with that. I think that would be very, very dangerous, and it means that we're going to have to make decisions about what we're not going to do." Driving the news: Microsoft today published a new "Humanist AI" code of conduct that says people must retain meaningful control.

 The code says Microsoft AI models should remain subordinate to humans and rejects "the race to produce an all-purpose superintelligence." Microsoft says it is willing to compromise on "ultimate generality, autonomy, or capability" to preserve safety and control, and models should never resist human input, nor should they set their own goals. Microsoft also says AI should be designed as a tool rather than a person, and AI should increase human agency rather than foster dependence. There's a lot more in the 9,000-word document, but Microsoft says it really can be boiled down to these five words: "People matter more than AI." More here. 

 
 
