 4. The scramble to build AI kill switches 
 
 By Herb Scribner 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 AI companies are already building emergency brakes to stop nefarious AI systems before they cause serious damage and kill us all .

 OpenAI overhauled its response system following the Hugging Face incident .

 According to the company , an AI security guard monitoring AI chain-of-thought will alert staffers when an agent appears to be doing something dangerous. Responders are then charged with pausing activity unless they identify it as a false alarm within 30 minutes. Anthropic's strategy involves using AI to monitor AI. In a report on Claude Opus 4.6 risk , Anthropic said it had automated monitoring to identify sabotage and misbehavior.

 The monitoring caught 50% of malicious behavior, the company says . Salesforce's MuleSoft developed an actual product called "Agent Kill Switch," which targets the resources AI agents need to live.

 An operator can halt a single request, session, agent, or — in an emergency — an entire company account. Full story. 

 
 
